Z||000000||varnster||03-15-2001||08:00 PM||||got the wheel off, the plastic plate off, the<BR>turn signel stock disconnected, hazard switch<BR>dissconnected, the two silver bolts taken out<BR>the little black screw taken out and the little white switch thingy off, now what?<BR>ive turned the key in different positions<BR>but the ignition switch still wont come out.<BR>help I IM AT THE END OF MY ROPE!||||reg||8
Z||000001||danny boy||03-15-2001||09:08 PM||||I just did this yesterday. If the black Torx bit screw is out and it still won't come out,there is a groove on the bottom of the switch.Make sure the key is off and wiggle the whole thing around.It may not be lined up correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||varnster||03-15-2001||10:37 PM||||danny boy, got the switch out now comes the<BR>next problem you know the big spring that<BR>is on the stearing shaft, when I reasembled <BR>everything the spring seems to have sprung <BR>i put the black cover on but it takes everything I can do to compress it down enough to see the snap ring grove much less put it back on, is this normal? when I first took the black cover off, it had no spring pressure on it I had to pull it off. I must <BR>be doing something wrong. their is nothing <BR>in the archives explaning this procedure or<BR>in the haynes manual. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||danny boy||03-17-2001||12:07 AM||||I pushed it in as far as I could and held it while pressing the snapring with a screwdriver. get one of the ends started and then work your way around til its all the way on. If you're really flexible use your feet to hold it in place. Also check to see if the bearing on the shaft seated all the way.<P>  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stealth_feb2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||03-17-2001||10:31 AM||||[QUyOTE]Originally posted by varnster:<BR><B>danny boy, got the switch out now comes the<BR>next problem you know the big spring that<BR>is on the stearing shaft, when I reasembled <BR>everything the spring seems to have sprung <BR>i put the black cover on but it takes everything I can do to compress it down enough to see the snap ring grove much less put it back on, is this normal? when I first took the black cover off, it had no spring pressure on it I had to pull it off. I must <BR>be doing something wrong. their is nothing <BR>in the archives explaning this procedure or<BR>in the haynes manual. </B>[/QUOTE]<BR>you have a sping compressor for that lock plate I bet auto zone would have one that you could use or rent I dont remember but with two people you should be able to push the ring down far enough with your to thumbs and while holding the ring in place have other person push snap ring in place hope this helps good luck<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000005||varnster||03-17-2001||08:28 PM||||got her done, I bought a lock plate tool at advance auto for 14 bucks it took all of two<BR>min with the cool little tool, any one going<BR>behind that black plate must have this tool<BR>it is great, cheap, and saves on the ole hands.||||reg||1
